                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_undersold:     `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Racine is capital-efficient but under-resourced on sales — there is pipeline, but no enterprise sales muscle to convert it. The stall isn't the product or the mission; it's that growth was never properly resourced. That reframes every funding path as a question of <em>what to resource, and at what cost to who you are.</em>`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>“The product has peaked” is the comfortable misdiagnosis.</strong> A €12M-funded rival in the same market says otherwise. The stall is a resourcing problem — pipeline you can't convert without sales muscle — not a ceiling. Call it a product wall and you'll fund-raise to fix the wrong thing.`,
                    coach_r1_mission:       `<strong>Blaming the mission is exactly the trap to avoid.</strong> The two clients you turned away are why your team and anchor customers trust you — that selectivity is the moat, not the brake. Treat the mission as the problem and you'll trade away your real competitive advantage.`,
                    coach_r1_luck:          `<strong>“It will recover on its own” is how nine months of runway becomes a crisis.</strong> Hope is not a growth plan, and the competitor's €12M won't wait. Name the real cause — under-resourced sales — and the runway clock — or you'll be raising from weakness by Q4.`,
                    coach_r1_plenty:        `<strong>“Plenty of time” misreads the clock.</strong> €855K ÷ €95K is about nine months, and you need a quarter to run a raise and a quarter of buffer — so the decision is <em>now</em>. Underestimate the clock and you fund-raise from weakness while the competitor spends.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Runway is not a finance detail — it is the constraint that times every decision.</strong> At €95K burn, every month you don't decide is €95K of optionality gone. “Growth first, money follows” is how founders raise from desperation. Read the clock first.`,
                    coach_r1_identity:      `<strong>The right two to defend.</strong> Mission selectivity and team mission-fit are what make customers trust you and engineers stay — the moat a term sheet can quietly dissolve. Naming them now means you'll recognise the clause that would cost them later.`,
                    coach_r1_growthkeep:    `<strong>Naming growth and valuation as your identity is the seed of the VC trap.</strong> A high valuation is a number; the identity that retains your team and anchor customers is the asset that produced the revenue. Defend the moat, not the multiple.`,
                    coach_r1_nonekeep:      `<strong>“Nothing is sacred under survival pressure” is how you survive into irrelevance.</strong> If identity is the first thing you trade, you'll sign the clause that drives out the engineers and cools the customers who were your growth. The moat is exactly what you protect under pressure.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Under-resourced sales, nine months on the clock, and the two identity attributes — mission selectivity and team mission-fit — named as non-negotiable. That is the lens that lets you read every term sheet for what it really costs.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's the network as a growth instrument.</strong> Warm pilots and co-marketing your cohort already offered, backed by enough founder attention to land them — and you still protected time to run the raise. You've substituted relationship capital for cash <em>and</em> strengthened the metrics every funder will price.`,
                    coach_r2_skip:          `<strong>You went straight to fund-raising and left the network on the table.</strong> Three warm enterprise pilots your cohort offered, unused — that's €400–600K of resourcefulness and the very metrics that would have strengthened your raise and your negotiating position. Why pitch strangers before activating the people who already trust you?`,
                    coach_r2_under:         `<strong>You touched the network but barely funded it with attention.</strong> A pilot you don't show up for doesn't close. With this little founder time on it, most of the €400–600K stays on the table. Network value is real only when it's actually activated.`,
                    coach_r2_over:          `<strong>Lovely momentum — and you starved the raise.</strong> A whole quarter on co-marketing and warm intros is high network leverage, but the funding clock kept ticking and your burn climbed. Split the founder's attention deliberately, or you'll leverage the network straight into a desperation raise.`,
                    coach_r3_vctrap:        `<strong>Funded at €24M — and the moat just cracked.</strong> Accepting the “any-customer” clause and the 2x preference reads to your team and anchor customers as a betrayal of the thesis. Two quarters out, your best mission-hired engineers leave and an anchor customer cools. Identity-alignment collapsed from 82 toward 41. <em>What did the valuation actually cost you?</em>`,
                    coach_r3_vcrenegotiate: `<strong>That's funding-path fit, not valuation tunnel vision.</strong> You took the capital but defended mission selectivity — dropping the “any-customer” clause and softening the preference. The best term sheet is the one whose strings you can live with, not the highest number. Your network leverage is exactly what made the renegotiation credible.`,
                    coach_r3_vcreject:      `<strong>Rejecting every string kills the VC deal.</strong> A €6M Series A always carries some terms; “no strings at all” simply means no raise — and now you're back to the €12M competitor with no new capital. Renegotiating the identity-violating terms beats walking from the capital entirely.`,
                    coach_r3_rbf:           `<strong>Revenue-based finance keeps the identity and the cap table.</strong> €1.5M, no dilution, a cash-flow claim instead of a board seat — slower, but yours. It only works if your network leverage is doing the growth work the VC's millions would have. Make sure the rate you target is one this lighter capital can actually carry.`,
                    coach_r3_bootstrapblind: `<strong>You kept 100% of your soul — and a €12M rival is outspending you on your own enterprise deals.</strong> Identity purism without the network to compensate leaves ARR flat near €1.4M and runway thinning. Was identity worth losing the market? There was a path that protected both.`,
                    coach_r3_partnertrap:   `<strong>The €2M and the distribution looked free.</strong> Then the exclusivity clause blocked the retail channel that was 30% of your pipeline, and the board seat now vetoes your next raise. You traded independence for one customer's roadmap. <em>What would you have negotiated out before signing?</em>`,
                    coach_r3_partnerrenegotiate: `<strong>You priced the strings before signing.</strong> Capping the exclusivity so it doesn't block the retail channel and limiting the board seat turns “free money” into a genuine distribution deal. The capital's headline price was never the real cost — the control was.`,
                    coach_r3_partnerreject: `<strong>Rejecting all terms walks away from the partnership.</strong> Distribution deals always carry some dependence; “no strings” means no deal and no distribution. Negotiating the exclusivity and board seat down would have kept the upside without the trap.`,
                    coach_r3_held:          `<strong>You held the line under real pressure.</strong> The accelerated deadline and the hardened exclusivity were designed to make you trade identity for speed — and you renegotiated instead of caving, with your network as the fallback if a deal slipped. That's how a venture raises from strength.`,
                    coach_r4_hirenow:       `<strong>You funded the growth the runway is for.</strong> Committing the €180K enterprise sales lead now means the two enterprise deals every path assumed actually close this half. Yes, burn rises — but protecting cash by starving the growth case is the false economy the runway exists to avoid.`,
                    coach_r4_hiredelay:     `<strong>You saved €30K of burn and slipped the pipeline a quarter.</strong> Every growth path assumed that sales lead closing two deals this half — delay the hire and you've starved the very growth the cash was for. <em>When is not spending the riskier choice?</em>`,
                    coach_r4_hirenever:     `<strong>No enterprise sales lead at all is a bet that the founder can be everywhere.</strong> Warm pilots open doors; they don't close enterprise contracts on their own. Skip the hire and the growth case every path assumed simply doesn't materialise.`,
                    coach_r4_credible:      `<strong>That's growth on purpose.</strong> A credible €3M ARR milestone at a rate your resources, identity and network can carry — funded by the sales hire and the network, not by a velocity that breaks the team. This is the plan a board can believe and the venture can survive.`,
                    coach_r4_hyper:         `<strong>Chasing 3x at any cost is the rate that breaks the venture.</strong> Hyper-growth your resources can't carry burns cash, strains the team and tempts you back toward the identity-violating clause. Sustainable growth is a rate, not a maximum — and the board can tell the difference.`,
                    coach_r4_flat:          `<strong>Playing it too safe defers the milestone past your next-raise window.</strong> Conserving cash by barely growing leaves you below €3M ARR when you need to raise again — and the €12M competitor doesn't pause. Slow is a failure mode too.`,
                    coach_r4_convergence:   `<strong>That's the board-ready narrative.</strong> Growth, runway, network leverage and identity-alignment converging on a rate Racine can sustain — and you owned the trade-off you made rather than hiding behind one number. That's the story that earns the next raise from strength.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchval:      `<strong>Leading with the valuation invites the obvious probe.</strong> “Great multiple — but your best engineers left and an anchor cooled; what is that growth actually built on?” A number without an intact moat is a story a board stops believing.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchharmony:  `<strong>Leading with mission purity invites the other probe.</strong> “You protected identity — so why is ARR flat while the competitor scales?” Purity that never converts into sustainable growth still loses the market. The board wants both, converging.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed under-resourced sales, read the 9-month clock, named the moat.`,
